<LegalLayout
  title="About CurlToCode | Who Builds It and How It Works"
  description="CurlToCode is an independently built cURL converter that runs entirely in your browser. How it works, why it refuses some conversions, and who maintains it."
  eyebrow="About"
  heading="About CurlToCode"
  lede="An independently built tool for turning cURL commands into working code, and code back into cURL, without sending anything to a server."
  updated="2026-08-20"
  crumb="About"
  path="/about"
>
  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>
    CurlToCode converts a cURL command into idiomatic source code for the HTTP
    client of your choice — currently 71 generator targets across 37 language
    and format families — and reads statically analysable code from 52 of them
    back into cURL. It is free, needs no account, and works offline once the
    page has loaded.
  </p>

  <h2>Who builds it</h2>
  <p>
    CurlToCode is an independent project built and maintained by a single
    developer. It is not a company, and it is not affiliated with the cURL
    project, with Google, or with any of the language and library vendors whose
    tools it generates code for.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    Your command is tokenized with a shell-aware tokenizer, parsed into a
    normalized HTTP request — method, URL, query, headers, cookies,
    authentication, and body — and then handed to a generator for the client you
    picked. Every generator reads that same model, which is why the request that
    reaches your server is the same one cURL would have sent, whichever language
    you choose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Conversion in the other direction reads your code as an abstract syntax tree
    rather than running it. A value that only exists at runtime is reported as
    an unresolved expression instead of being guessed at.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it sometimes refuses</h2>
  <p>
    Some clients genuinely cannot express some requests: duplicate header names
    in a Python dictionary, a multipart body in the JDK HTTP client, a local
    file path in browser JavaScript. When that happens the converter tells you
    so, rather than emitting code that quietly sends something different from
    what you asked for. A refusal is a feature — it is the difference between a
    tool you can trust with production traffic and one you cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Why everything runs in your browser</h2>
  <p>
    cURL commands are full of secrets. Bearer tokens, API keys, basic auth
    passwords, and session cookies are exactly the things people paste into a
    converter, and exactly the things that should not be uploaded to a
    stranger's server. Running conversion locally removes the question entirely:
    there is no server to trust, because there is no server involved. Our <a
      href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a
    > covers this in detail.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is funded</h2>
  <p>
    The site is free and carries advertising. Advertising pays for the domain
    and the time that goes into maintaining the generators. It has no access to
    what you paste into the converter — it reads the page, not the contents of a
    text box.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    Bug reports, a client you would like supported, or a conversion that came
    out wrong: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. See the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a> for what is most useful to include.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
